<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Description>
      FlowX Redis lease store: time-bounded exclusive ownership of a flow instance and the
      monotonic fencing tokens that survive both a clean release and an expiry.
    </Description>
    <PackageTags>flowx;redis;lease;fencing-token;durable-execution;leader-election</PackageTags>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Pinned exactly, like every other dependency in this repository. The client is the
      only thing this package needs beyond the contracts: ADR-0009 says a plugin depends
      on FlowX.Abstractions and on whatever it talks to, and nothing else.

      The surface used here is the static, allocation-conscious half of the library —
      IDatabase.ScriptEvaluateAsync with explicit RedisKey/RedisValue arrays — and no
      part of it is reflective, which is what keeps constraint C2 satisfied without a
      suppression.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="StackExchange.Redis" Version="3.1.0" />
    <!--
      10.0.5 rather than the 10.0.0 the rest of the repository pins, and the difference is
      forced rather than chosen: StackExchange.Redis 3.1.0 resolves
      Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions 10.0.5, which requires this package at 10.0.5
      or above. Declaring 10.0.0 here is a package downgrade, which NU1605 makes an error.
      Pinned to the version the graph actually resolves, so the .csproj states what is
      restored instead of a number that is quietly overruled.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="10.0.5" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The same rule FlowX.Postgres states: a plugin depends on the runtime, the runtime
      never depends on a plugin (RuntimeDoesNotReferenceAnyPlugin). This one depends on
      less than that. ILeaseStore, FlowLease, FencingToken, Result and Error all live in
      FlowX.Abstractions, so a lease store needs nothing from Core, Runtime or Hosting —
      and nothing at all from plugins/FlowX.Postgres, which is the point of the package:
      two adapters that share a suite and no source.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/FlowX.Abstractions/FlowX.Abstractions.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
